---
import CaseStudyLayout from "@layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import CaseStudyHero from "@components/CaseStudyHero.astro";
import CaseStudyMeta from "@components/CaseStudyMeta.astro";
import Figure from "@components/Figure.astro";
import Pull from "@components/Pull.astro";
import ProcessColumns from "@components/ProcessColumns.astro";
import Pivot from "@components/Pivot.astro";
import Strip from "@components/Strip.astro";
import ImagePair from "@components/ImagePair.astro";
import Metrics from "@components/Metrics.astro";
import NextCaseStudy from "@components/NextCaseStudy.astro";

import hpe01 from "@img/hpe-01.webp";
import hpe02 from "@img/hpe-02.webp";
import hpe03 from "@img/hpe-03.webp";
import hpe04 from "@img/hpe-04.webp";
import hpe05 from "@img/hpe-05.webp";
import hpe06 from "@img/hpe-06.webp";
import hpe07 from "@img/hpe-07.webp";
import hpe09 from "@img/hpe-09.webp";
import hpe10 from "@img/hpe-10.webp";
import hpe11 from "@img/hpe-11.webp";
import hpe12 from "@img/hpe-12.webp";
import hpe13 from "@img/hpe-13.webp";

const title = "An AI troubleshooting agent for HPE";
const description =
	"Ten out of ten interviews wanted something leadership hadn't asked for: a way to know whether a pipeline failure was theirs before they had to ask an engineer.";
---

<CaseStudyLayout title={title} description={description}>
	<Fragment slot="hero">
	<CaseStudyHero eyebrow={["Product design", "HPE", "Agentic AI for ML pipelines"]} title="Debugging's hardest step was asking for help">
		Leadership wanted an AI feature. Ten out of ten interviews wanted something else: data scientists on
		small teams felt guilty, not stuck, when a pipeline burning tens of thousands of dollars a day failed and
		they couldn't help the engineer fix it. So the AI we built in one week reads the logs and answers the one
		question a data scientist usually had to ask an engineer to find out: whether the failure was theirs, in
		how the pipeline was set up, or the infrastructure's, in the systems running it underneath. The proof of
		concept shipped on a Friday. The following week, the whole department was laid off, and the test that
		would have proved it never ran.
	</CaseStudyHero>

	<CaseStudyMeta
		items={[
			{ label: "Role", value: "Product designer, solo" },
			{ label: "Team", value: "One developer intern, one language model wired to live pipeline logs" },
			{ label: "Timeline", value: "1 week" },
			{ label: "Research base", value: "10 user interviews, 1 cross-functional workshop" },
		]}
	/>
	</Fragment>

	<Figure
		src={hpe01}
		alt="The Pachyderm job log viewer: a failed job's timestamped logs in the center, and an Explain Logs panel open on the right showing a GPT-3.5 chat that identifies a schema mismatch between the GlotusDB source and the KryptonProcessor pipeline, and recommends checking the data contracts."
		figNum="Fig. 01"
		lead="A shared starting point."
		wide
		fit="contain"
		deck
		deckLabel="Shipped"
	>
		The assistant sits where debugging already happens. It tells a data scientist whether the failure is
		theirs to fix or the engineer's, so they arrive at the engineer's desk with an answer instead of a
		question.
	</Figure>

	<section id="problem">
		<div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">01</span><h2>The problem</h2><span class="sec-rule"></span></div>
		<p>
			For years the product team had chased one goal: make the web app do everything the command line
			could. Parity felt like progress, but it quietly favored the people who lived in the terminal, the ML
			engineers, and left their closest partners, the data scientists, with an interface built for someone
			else. That mattered commercially as much as it did for the people using it. HPE's strategy was to
			land with one team and expand to the next, and an engineer who can't win over the data scientist
			beside them isn't going to win over the team down the hall.
		</p>
		<p>
			The cost of a bad handoff was concrete. Running a pipeline could cost tens of thousands of dollars a
			day, a typical run lasted four to seven days, and usage told the story of when people actually needed
			the product: hundreds of sessions a week while creating or debugging a pipeline, and one to ten a week
			once it was stable. <strong class="deck-stat" data-deck-foot="Product usage, week over week|HPE ML pipelines"
				>People showed up when something broke, and the interface was least ready for them right
				then.</strong
			> Into that, leadership arrived with a clean, reasonable ask: ship an AI feature. Roadmap planning for
			the next quarter was open, and there was room for something riskier.
		</p>
		<ImagePair
			mid
			deckLabel="The handoff"
			items={[
				{
					fn: "Fig. 02",
					imgSrc: hpe02,
					imgAlt: "The old right panel during the same failure: pipeline name, job ID, and roles at the top, then a Subjob Summary reporting the job Failed — 106 total datums, 45 processed, 32 skipped, 19 failed, 10 recovered — with no explanation of what went wrong or what to do next.",
					fit: "contain",
					caption:
						'<strong class="fig-lead">Built for the terminal, viewed in a browser.</strong>The panel reported 19 of 106 datums failed. It never said why, or what to do next. For a data scientist, that gap was the wall.',
				},
				{
					fn: "Fig. 03",
					imgSrc: hpe03,
					imgAlt: "A simplified diagram of the two roles around the AI model: the data scientist supplies business context, datasets, tuning, and training; the ML engineer builds and maintains the infrastructure, feature engineering, and scaling underneath a model running at roughly $10k a day.",
					fit: "contain",
					caption:
						'<strong class="fig-lead">Two roles, one expensive model.</strong>The data scientist supplies business context and data. The engineer builds and keeps the infrastructure running underneath it. Between them sits a model burning about $10k a day, and each side only sees half of it.',
				},
			]}
		/>
	</section>

	<section id="research">
		<div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">02</span><h2>What we found</h2><span class="sec-rule"></span></div>
		<p>
			Ten interviews, ten people naming the same thing without being asked. Miscommunication between data
			scientists and ML engineers came up in every conversation, and unanimity like that changes a brief:
			you're no longer looking at a symptom, you're looking at the disease. What surprised me more was the
			emotional register. Data scientists didn't say they lacked tools. They said they felt guilty, that
			they were letting a teammate down, and their verbs were relationship verbs: ping, interrupt, bother.
		</p>
		<Pull
			quote="It's like having blood work done, and having a doctor with good bedside manner explain your test results in a way that you can easily understand."
			attribution="Workshop participant, describing what they wanted debugging to feel like"
		/>
		<Figure
			src={hpe04}
			alt="A two-lane journey map from the research wall. Initial Setup: requirements gathering, data dictionary, development, QA. Debugging: identify the component, root cause analysis, defect management — each step paired with the friction point the workshop surfaced for it."
			figNum="Fig. 04"
			lead="The failure moment, mapped."
			wide
			fit="contain"
			deck
			deckLabel="Journey"
		>
			Every card in the debugging lane pointed the same way: the pain peaked not when the pipeline broke,
			but when someone had to admit they couldn't read why.
		</Figure>
		<p>
			The team had just spent real money expanding the documentation and wiring ChatGPT to it, and
			leadership hoped the docs could work as a training manual, teaching people to build pipelines that
			failed less. The investment was sound, but its message to a stressed data scientist was "this is how
			you get better at the product." <strong
				class="deck-stat"
				data-deck-label="Guilt, not tools"
				data-deck-foot="What ten out of ten interviews surfaced|HPE">The pain wasn't "I lack tools." It was "I'm
				letting my teammate down."</strong
			> That is a relationship problem, and relationship problems don't get fixed by adding features. They get
			fixed by lowering the social cost of asking for help.
		</p>
		<Figure
			src={hpe05}
			alt="A workshop synthesis card in three parts: the pain point of needing a translator between data scientists and the line of business, the emotional core comparing the ideal to blood work explained with a doctor's good bedside manner and naming the guilt data scientists felt debugging a $10k-a-day AI model, and the resulting need statement about communicating pipeline failures with confidence instead of guilt."
			figNum="Fig. 05"
			lead="Bedside manner as a spec."
			wide
			fit="contain"
			deck
			deckLabel="Research highlight"
		>
			One participant wanted "a translator to go between us." Another compared it to blood work, wanting a
			doctor's bedside manner in how results get explained. That language, not a feature list, became the
			spec.
		</Figure>
	</section>

	<section id="approach">
		<div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">03</span><h2>Three decisions in one week</h2><span class="sec-rule"></span></div>
		<p>A week leaves no room for a bake-off. Three decisions had to be made early and held, and each one pushed against the easy version of the brief.</p>
		<ProcessColumns
			deckLabel="Three decisions"
			deckCaption="Reframe the problem, run the workshop through a behavioral lens, and build something real enough to test trust."
			items={[
				{
					stepLabel: "Decision 01",
					frameN: "01",
					title: "The reframe",
					imgSrc: hpe06,
					imgAlt: "A research synthesis board: Pain Points quotes about HPE's steep learning curve on the left, a bank of open-ended AI Implementation Value ideas on the right, and a Reframe panel below crossing out 'Build an AI tool.' and replacing it with 'Build a tool that helps users gain clarity into opaque AI systems and infrastructure.'",
					body: '"Build an AI tool" could mean anything and measure nothing. I crossed it out and wrote the real target next to it: build a tool that helps users gain clarity into opaque AI systems and infrastructure. AI stayed the answer; the question changed, from what feature to ship to what a person actually can\'t see, and every decision after this one got sharper for it.',
				},
				{
					stepLabel: "Decision 02",
					frameN: "02",
					title: "The lens",
					imgSrc: hpe07,
					imgAlt: "A Big Ideas workshop board: a mocked-up 'Welcome back' events screen listing failed pipelines on the left, and on the right an idea note reading 'It's like concierge for my work: take the pressure off of having to find where this has gone wrong, and provide me ways to fix the problem,' scored Feasibility: Low and Desirability: High.",
					body: "Big Ideas alone rewards novelty. Scoring each concept for feasibility and desirability kept the wide-open ones honest, and this one scored low on feasibility and high on desirability, exactly the kind of idea a novelty-only round would have buried. I took it forward anyway, then ran it through the Behavioral Design Toolkit from the Illinois Institute of Technology on my own, to pressure-test whether it would actually change how someone worked, not just whether they liked the sound of it.",
				},
				{
					stepLabel: "Decision 03",
					frameN: "03",
					title: "The build",
					imgAlt: "The same job log viewer and Explain Logs panel from the hero, cropped tighter: a GPT-3.5 chat identifying a schema mismatch between GlotusDB and the KryptonProcessor pipeline, answering live against the real pipeline logs shown at left.",
					placeholderLabel: "Code it, don't mock it",
					body: "A clickable Figma would have tested layout and copy. The riskiest unknown was whether people would trust the AI's answers, and trust only shows up when the answers are real. I argued for a coded proof of concept, wired to a live model reading HPE's actual pipeline logs, so what testers saw would be a real answer, not a scripted one.",
				},
			]}
		/>

		<Pivot heading="Guilt isn't fixed with features. It's fixed by making it cheaper to ask.">
			The result was a flow on rails. An "explain logs" button sits exactly where a person already is when
			a pipeline fails, and one tap returns a plain-language summary of what went wrong, including the one
			thing that used to take a conversation to figure out: whether the fault was in the pipeline itself or
			in the infrastructure underneath it. What it changes is the conversation between the two roles, from
			"I don't know how to fix this" to "here's what I know, and this is where I need your help." The
			assistant doesn't replace the engineer. It makes the walk to the engineer's desk shorter, and a lot
			less costly to take.
		</Pivot>

		<Strip
			land
			deckLabel="The flow"
			figNum="Fig. 06"
			lead="Where the fault lives, first."
			items={[
				{
					frameN: "01",
					dataLabel: "See where the fault lives",
					imgSrc: hpe09,
					imgAlt: "The pipeline dashboard for TRAIN_DATA_queries, showing a healthy pipeline next to a failed subjob, with View Logs and Pipeline Actions in the panel beside it.",
				},
				{
					frameN: "02",
					dataLabel: "Open Explain Logs",
					imgSrc: hpe10,
					imgAlt: "The job's log screen, listing the failed extraction steps, with the cursor clicking the Explain Logs button in the panel on the right.",
				},
				{
					frameN: "03",
					dataLabel: "Point it at your own model",
					imgSrc: hpe11,
					imgAlt: "The Explain Logs setup panel, asking for an OpenAI API token, or for teams with their own LLM, a model URL and token instead.",
				},
			]}
		>
			The dashboard already separates a healthy pipeline from a failed subjob, so a person knows before
			opening a single log whether the break is in HPE's app or the infrastructure underneath it. From
			there, one click opens the explainer in the same screen, and it can run on the company's own model
			instead of sending anything out.
		</Strip>

		<ImagePair
			deckLabel="Before / after"
			items={[
				{
					fn: "Before",
					imgSrc: hpe12,
					imgAlt: "A dense table of raw, timestamped Pachyderm job logs, unformatted and uninterpreted.",
					fit: "contain",
					caption: "Readable if you already knew what you were looking for. A wall if you didn't.",
				},
				{
					fn: "After",
					imgSrc: hpe13,
					imgAlt: "The same job's Explain Logs panel: a GPT-3.5 answer identifying the schema mismatch behind the failure, in plain language.",
					fit: "contain",
					caption: "The same failure, translated. Bedside manner, in software.",
				},
			]}
		/>
	</section>

	<section id="outcome">
		<div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">04</span><h2>Outcome</h2><span class="sec-rule"></span></div>
		<p>What shipped, in the week we had:</p>
		<Metrics
			three
			items={[
				{ n: "10 / 10", label: "Interviews that named miscommunication between the two roles as a major issue, unprompted" },
				{ n: "1 week", label: "From research readout to a coded proof of concept, live and answering against real pipeline logs" },
				{ n: "1 Live AI Agent", label: "Coded from day one, so what testers saw was a real answer, not a scripted one" },
			]}
		/>
		<p>
			The proof of concept was real enough to test the thing that mattered, whether people would trust the
			assistant's answers, and it was ready for in-depth user testing against a behavior-change spec, not
			just a usability checklist. One gap was known and scoped for the next release: the assistant explained
			failures but didn't yet walk a person through fixing them.
		</p>
		<p>
			Then the ending most case studies don't have. <strong
				class="deck-stat"
				data-deck-label="What happened"
				data-deck-foot="The week after the PoC shipped|HPE AI Solutions"
				>The PoC shipped on a Friday. Within the week, the entire AI Solutions department, this team
				included, was laid off.</strong
			> The user test that would have validated or challenged the hypothesis never happened. What did happen,
			and what I'd argue was the larger outcome anyway, was that a product team stopped thinking of AI as a showcase
			and started thinking of it as a way to support someone through a bad moment.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="reflection">
		<div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">05</span><h2>Reflection</h2><span class="sec-rule"></span></div>
		<p>
			The most valuable things this project produced were not the prototype. They were the reframe and the
			insight behind it, that guilt isn't solved with features but by lowering the social cost of asking for
			help. If I'd known how little runway was left, I would have spent less of the week polishing the
			coded build and more of it writing that research down in a form that could travel on its own,
			independent of the team that produced it.
		</p>
		<p>
			Good research outlives bad timing, but only if someone builds it to. The artifact never got the
			chance to prove itself. The thinking behind it should have been packaged to survive the people who
			built it, and on every project since, the research readout has been written as if the team might not
			be there to present it.
		</p>
	</section>

	<NextCaseStudy slot="close" href="/case-studies/quantalyric-mvp" label="Forty hours, one chart worth funding" />
</CaseStudyLayout>
